Q: Hibernate LEFT JOIN FETCH returns empty PersistentBag in Spring @Transactional integration test A: <p>The &quot;empty PersistentBag&quot; issue in a @Transactional context might be caused by Persistence Context Pollution (First-Level Cache). Let's try to force Hibernate to &quot;forget&quot; the cached entities so it must use the results of your JOIN FETCH query.</p>
<pre><code>@PersistenceContext
private EntityManager entityManager;

@Transactional
public void yourMethod() {
    // ... previous logic that might have loaded the entity ...
    entityManager.clear(); 
    List&lt;Parent&gt; results = repository.findAllWithChildren();
}
</code></pre>

Q: Can I determine if an argument is string literal? A: <p>C++26 introduces <code>std::is_string_literal</code> from <code>&lt;meta&gt;</code> exactly for this!</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;meta&gt;

int main()
{
    static_assert(std::is_string_literal(&quot;foo&quot;));
}
</code></pre>
<p>Available in GCC-16/trunk</p>
<p>If you want to use it with older standards, use <code>__builtin_is_string_literal</code> which is underlying compiler builtin used to implement this function.</p>
<p><a href="https://gcc.godbolt.org/z/jvvGWG6MW" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>

Q: RawInput WinAPI: GetRawInputBuffer() and message handling A: <p>The root cause of your inconsistent results is a subtle but important requirement of <code>GetRawInputBuffer</code>: <strong>you must first consume the event carried by the <code>WM_INPUT</code> message via <code>GetRawInputData</code> before <code>GetRawInputBuffer</code> will see any remaining queued events.</strong></p>
<p>Each <code>WM_INPUT</code> message carries an <code>HRAWINPUT</code> handle in <code>lParam</code> — this is the first pending event. <code>GetRawInputBuffer</code> reads the thread's raw input queue, but this first event is not visible to it until the <code>WM_INPUT</code> message has been processed. This behavior is <strong>not documented</strong> on the <code>GetRawInputBuffer</code> page.</p>
<p>The correct pattern is:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>case WM_INPUT:
{
    // Step 1: consume the first event via GetRawInputData
    UINT bufferSize = myBufferSize;
    UINT result = GetRawInputData(
        reinterpret_cast&lt;HRAWINPUT&gt;(lParam),
        RID_INPUT, pBuffer, &amp;bufferSize, sizeof(RAWINPUTHEADER));

    if (result != (UINT)-1)
        ProcessInput(reinterpret_cast&lt;RAWINPUT*&gt;(pBuffer));

    // Step 2: drain remaining events that accumulated in the queue
    for (;;)
    {
        bufferSize = myBufferSize;
        UINT count = GetRawInputBuffer(
            reinterpret_cast&lt;RAWINPUT*&gt;(pBuffer),
            &amp;bufferSize, sizeof(RAWINPUTHEADER));

        if (count == 0)
            break; // queue empty

        if (count == (UINT)-1)
        {
            // buffer too small, grow and retry
            myBufferSize *= 2;
            pBuffer = realloc(pBuffer, myBufferSize);
            continue;
        }

        RAWINPUT* input = reinterpret_cast&lt;RAWINPUT*&gt;(pBuffer);
        for (UINT i = 0; i &lt; count; ++i, input = NEXTRAWINPUTBLOCK(input))
            ProcessInput(input);
        // don't break — there may be more
    }

    // Step 3: let DefWindowProc release the HRAWINPUT handle
    return DefWindowProc(hWnd, uMsg, wParam, lParam);
}
</code></pre>
<p>This explains all three symptoms you observed:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>approach #1 (Sleep)</strong> worked because by the time <code>GetRawInputBuffer</code> was called, all the <code>WM_INPUT</code> messages had already been dispatched through <code>DefWindowProc</code> which released their handles, making all events visible to <code>GetRawInputBuffer</code></li>
<li><strong>approach #2 (GetMessage)</strong> was unreliable because you called <code>GetRawInputBuffer</code> from inside <code>WndProc</code> before <code>DefWindowProc</code> had a chance to process the message</li>
<li><strong>approach #3 (PeekMessage + PM_NOREMOVE)</strong> worked consistently for the same reason as Sleep — messages were being dispatched before you drained the buffer</li>
</ul>
<p>Two additional notes:</p>
<p><strong>Buffer alignment</strong>: <code>GetRawInputBuffer</code> requires the buffer to be aligned to pointer size (<code>sizeof(void*)</code>). Use aligned allocation rather than <code>GetMem</code>/<code>malloc</code>.</p>
<p><strong><code>DefRawInputProc</code></strong> is indeed a no-op as you suspected — the real cleanup happens in <code>DefWindowProc</code> when it handles <code>WM_INPUT</code>. You can safely ignore <code>DefRawInputProc</code>.</p>
